## Recommended Batch 20 candidates — pick one (or propose your own)

### Direction E — Xendit-for-guests refactor (T2 ~18-25K, RECOMMENDED next)
Logical continuation of Direction A. Customer chooses "Pay with Xendit" on checkout →
order created + Xendit invoice created in same transaction → client redirects to Xendit
payment URL. Currently impossible because createXenditInvoice is writeProcedure (protected).
- Approach: extract Xendit invoice-creation logic from createXenditInvoice writeProcedure
  into a shared server-side helper `apps/web/src/lib/xendit-invoice.ts` (createXenditInvoiceForOrder).
- Both createXenditInvoice (admin path) AND placeOrderAsCustomer (guest path) call the helper.
- placeOrderAsCustomer adds optional `paymentMethod: "xendit"` to its enum; when chosen,
  the procedure creates the order then immediately creates the Xendit invoice in the same
  $transaction, returns {orderId, orderNumber, invoiceUrl}.
- Checkout form: 3rd radio option "Pay online via Xendit"; on success with invoiceUrl,
  redirect to invoiceUrl instead of /products.
- Tests: add 3-4 tests covering xendit path (success creates invoice + persists xenditPaymentId,
  failure on Xendit side rolls back order via transaction, paymentMethod="xendit" + missing
  Xendit config rejects gracefully).
- Branch: feat/batch-20-xendit-for-guests
- ⚠ Pre-flight: verify TenantXenditConfig table has the test-key fallback path so dev
  works without real keys. Confirm rateLimiters.public is the right tier for the
  Xendit-creating path (it currently is — same 30/min/IP for the whole procedure).

### Direction B — Mobile Expo scaffold Phase 1a (T2-T3, ~40-60K)
Inspect apps/mobile/src/ first to determine true starting state. package.json has deps
(expo + expo-router + watermelondb + async-storage + netinfo + expo-camera + expo-local-
authentication + expo-location + expo-notifications) but the src/ tree may already be
partially scaffolded. If genuinely from-zero, this batch deserves explicit sub-batch plan
in the BUILD BATCH PROPOSAL.

### Direction C — Production-readiness bundle for Xendit (T2, ~20-35K)
Item 1: Async webhook via BullMQ (packages/jobs has bullmq@^5.34.5 confirmed). Webhook
  enqueues a job, returns 200 immediately; worker processes the payment update.
Item 2: pino structured logging stack across server-side routes and webhook handler.
Item 3: Admin Settings → Integrations page for Xendit config visibility (read-only display
  of test/live key presence, last successful webhook timestamp, recent failure log).
Becomes urgent when first real test-key transaction happens in staging.

### Direction D — Small carry-forward bundle (T1-T2, ~15-25K combined)
Item 1: accounting.reverse $transaction wrap (CONFIRMED EXISTS at
  apps/web/src/server/trpc/routers/accounting.ts:213).
Item 2: AUTH_TRUST_HOST env propagation across env files + sync script.
Item 3 (optional): Quotation column reorder + template save/load.

### Direction F — Guest order tracking (T1-T2, ~12-18K)
Pairs with Direction A completion. Adds publicProcedure getOrderByNumber+email so guests
can check status without an account. New page /[slug]/store/orders/lookup. Useful before
Direction E ships because customers will want to confirm their order exists even on the
cash-on-delivery path.

## New recurring-pattern lessons surfaced during Batch 18 Item 2 (apply at next batch PRE-FLIGHT):

- 🟢 PUBLICPROCEDURE TENANT RESOLUTION — publicProcedure has no ctx.tenantId or ctx.tenantSlug.
  Input MUST carry tenantSlug; procedure resolves via db.tenant.findUnique({where:{slug}}).
  Default warehouse resolves server-side via db.warehouse.findFirst({where:{isDefault,isActive}}).
  NEVER let the client supply warehouseId on a public path — that's an authorization boundary.

- 🟢 SYSTEM-ACTOR LOOKUP FOR GUEST WRITES — any required FK to User on a public write path
  (StockMovement.createdById, AuditLog.userId, etc.) resolves via db.user.findFirst
  ({where:{isActive:true},orderBy:{createdAt:"asc"},select:{id:true}}) which returns the
  seeded webmaster. Reusable pattern for all future guest-attributed writes.

- 🟢 RATE-LIMIT TIER FOR PUBLIC ENDPOINTS — rateLimiters.public.check(ip) at 30/min/IP is
  the right tier for guest-facing mutations. IP extracted from
  ctx.req.headers.get("x-forwarded-for") ?? ctx.req.headers.get("x-real-ip") ?? "unknown".

- 🟢 SANITIZEPLAINTEXT FOR PUBLIC TEXT FIELDS — apply to firstName/lastName/notes (any user-
  facing text that may be rendered in admin views). Email passes through Zod email() validator
  which already strips HTML. Address fields go to Json columns (revisit if ever rendered as
  innerHTML somewhere).

- ⚖️ DUPLICATED ORDER-CREATION LOGIC — placeOrderAsCustomer duplicates ~80% of placeOrder body.
  NOT extracted to a shared helper because the divergences are real (no tax/shipping/discount
  fields, sanitization step, find-or-create customer, system-actor attribution, auto-resolved
  warehouse). Extraction premature until a 3rd variant emerges (e.g. POS sale checkout).

- 🔴 USECART() API SHAPE GOTCHA — the cart Provider exposes specific named actions; always
  grep the useMemo value before destructuring in a new consumer. This batch hit two typecheck
  errors before first test run: destructured `clearCart` (wrong, action is `clear`) and
  `tenantSlug` (wrong, not on context until this batch added it). Both fixed in follow-up
  edits. Lesson: BEFORE writing a new useCart consumer, run
  `grep -A20 "const value = useMemo" apps/web/src/lib/cart-store.tsx` to see the actual shape.

- 🟢 INTENTIONAL SCOPE-TRIM CONTINUES — Batch 18 Item 2 deliberately shipped without Xendit
  integration for guests (Option α chosen over Option β at proposal time). Reason: Xendit
  integration would require either extracting the Xendit-call logic from createXenditInvoice
  into a shared helper OR adding a 2nd publicProcedure with its own pattern — either adds 8-12K
  tokens and deserves a focused batch (Direction E). 2nd consecutive intentional scope-trim
  (Item 1 was 1st). Pattern: when a Direction bundles N items and one item touches a NEW
  security/integration/auth surface, prefer to ship that item in its own batch. Optimizes for
  review focus, not throughput.

